WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.620
Mary Poppins is back in Cherry Tree Lane,
so get ready to trip a little light fantastic

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.600
with everyone's favourite British nanny.

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:13.540
Yippee-ki-yay movie lovers, I'm Jan and today
I'm revealing the most supercalifragilisticexpialidocious

00:00:13.540 --> 00:00:18.700
easter eggs, nostalgic call-backs, cameos,
and secrets hidden in Mary Poppins Returns!

00:00:18.700 --> 00:00:22.160
If you spotted any others, do share them in
the comments below!

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.860
And be sure to subscribe to get all my new
Mary Poppins videos!

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:31.480
Some mild spoilers ahead if you haven't seen
the movie yet.

00:00:31.480 --> 00:00:35.540
During Lin-Manuel Miranda's opening number,
we get a mini-tour of London, with one iconic

00:00:35.540 --> 00:00:40.180
location – St Paul's Cathedral – making
a lovely return from the original movie.

00:00:40.180 --> 00:00:44.640
As the camera passes over the cathedral, look
carefully and you'll spot an old lady on the

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:49.620
steps and, as the camera moves up, a flock
of pigeons scatter and fly away.

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:54.790
This little moment is a very sweet homage
to the Bird Woman from the 1964 film who sold

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:58.050
breadcrumbs on the steps of St Paul's for
tuppence a bag.

00:00:58.050 --> 00:01:02.100
There's a lovely sequence in the new movie
where the children disappear down the bathtub

00:01:02.100 --> 00:01:05.190
drain with Mary as she sings "Can You Imagine
That?"

00:01:05.190 --> 00:01:09.750
This glorious fantasy sequence is a deep-cut
easter egg to an under-the-water scene based

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:14.070
on the books that didn't make it into the
first Mary Poppins film and it also has shades

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:19.310
of the under-the-sea sequence from Disney's
1971 movie Bedknobs and Broomsticks.

00:01:19.310 --> 00:01:23.600
And that connection is no coincidence because
the song "The Beautiful Briny", which Angela

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:28.010
Lansbury and David Tomlinson sing during their
watery adventures in Bedknobs and Broomsticks,

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:32.520
was actually originally written for 1964's
Mary Poppins.

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:36.260
And there's even more connections between
the movies because Angela Lansbury plays the

00:01:36.260 --> 00:01:40.800
part of the Balloon Lady in the Mary Poppins
sequel, and her fellow Bedknobs and Broomsticks

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.460
star David Tomlinson was the original Mr Banks.

00:01:43.460 --> 00:01:45.439
The "Can You Imagine That?"

00:01:45.439 --> 00:01:49.950
underwater scene and song also tip their hat
to Disney's Peter Pan [film] with a pirate

00:01:49.950 --> 00:01:53.510
ship appearing together with the line [in
the song] "Some pirates follow treasure maps

00:01:53.510 --> 00:01:55.409
[…] They search the world for buried gold.

00:01:55.409 --> 00:01:57.970
They won't grow up and don't grow old"

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:01.950
The animated English countryside sequence
from the original Mary Poppins is one of the

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:06.920
movie's most treasured moments, and the sequel
doesn't disappoint when it creates its own

00:02:06.920 --> 00:02:07.920
version.

00:02:07.920 --> 00:02:11.519
This time around, the family's Royal Doulton
Bowl serves as the portal into the fantasy

00:02:11.519 --> 00:02:16.230
world, with the painting on the bowl's surface
giving us a glimpse of what's inside the world,

00:02:16.230 --> 00:02:19.379
similar to Bert's pavement paintings in the
first film.

00:02:19.379 --> 00:02:23.560
The decision to employ old-school, hand-drawn
animation techniques for the scenes inside

00:02:23.560 --> 00:02:28.249
the bowl was a deliberate tribute to the original
movie by the sequel's director Rob Marshall.

00:02:28.249 --> 00:02:33.409
And to make Mary Poppins, Jack and the children
look like they really belonged in the 2D hand-drawn

00:02:33.409 --> 00:02:38.989
world, the costume design team literally painted
on top of the fabric used to make their costumes.

00:02:38.989 --> 00:02:43.579
The scale of production for these sequences
was immense with the entire process taking

00:02:43.579 --> 00:02:48.650
16 months to complete and involving more than
70 animators, some of whom even came out of

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:53.290
retirement for the opportunity to work on
a sequel to such a Disney classic.

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:58.040
A Royal Doulton Bowl also features in the
second Mary Poppins book, although what happens

00:02:58.040 --> 00:03:02.840
there is much darker and scarier as Jane ends
up trapped inside the bowl with a creepy old

00:03:02.840 --> 00:03:08.579
man who tells her she's gone back in time
60 years and he intends to keep her there!

00:03:08.579 --> 00:03:12.889
The Royal Doulton music hall is also the setting
for an especially inventive musical number

00:03:12.889 --> 00:03:17.090
called "A Cover Is Not The Book", in which
Mary and Jack perform a vaudeville song that's

00:03:17.090 --> 00:03:18.810
stacked with easter eggs to P.L.

00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:21.079
Travers' original Mary Poppins books.

00:03:21.079 --> 00:03:25.879
For example, the lyrics mention Nellie Rubina,
a human-sized wooden doll who gives the children

00:03:25.879 --> 00:03:29.669
Conversation Sweets in the book "Mary Poppins
Comes Back".

00:03:29.669 --> 00:03:33.779
Lin-Manuel Miranda gets to demonstrate the
kind of verbal dexterity that's become his

00:03:33.779 --> 00:03:38.860
signature in musicals like Hamilton when he
performs a 1930s-style rap as part of "A Cover

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:40.099
Is Not The Book".

00:03:40.099 --> 00:03:44.620
But Miranda's rap-esque moment is also a hat
tip to the rat-a-tat section in Jolly Holiday

00:03:44.620 --> 00:03:49.589
where Bert speak-sings about all the women
who can't quite match up to Mary Poppins.

00:03:49.589 --> 00:03:51.440
"Mavis and Sybil have ways that are winning."

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:53.909
"And Prudence and Gwendolyn set your heart
spinnin'"

00:03:53.909 --> 00:03:55.459
"Phoebe's delightful, Maude is disarming."

00:03:55.459 --> 00:03:57.010
"Felicia, Janice and Lydia"
"Charming!"

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:00.409
On the stage in the background, there's also
a book called "The Day Out", which is the

00:04:00.409 --> 00:04:04.969
title of the second chapter of PL Travers'
first Mary Poppins book, and that chapter

00:04:04.969 --> 00:04:08.290
was the inspiration for the "Jolly Holiday"
scene in the first movie.

00:04:08.290 --> 00:04:11.849
Another book in this scene called "The Cat
That Looked At A King" is an easter egg to

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:16.139
a chapter from the book, Mary Poppins Opens
The Door, which was also adapted into a short

00:04:16.139 --> 00:04:21.700
film starring Julie Andrews which appeared
on the 2004 DVD release of Mary Poppins.

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:26.790
The lovable penguins from the original film's
animated scenes make a comeback during the

00:04:26.790 --> 00:04:27.790
music hall section.

00:04:27.790 --> 00:04:31.410
The penguins – which danced with Bert in
the first film – this time around beg to

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:35.660
dance with Mary Poppins herself and later
they also dance with Jack.

00:04:35.660 --> 00:04:39.810
Mary even honks the beak of one of the penguins
which feels like a fun nod to how one of the

00:04:39.810 --> 00:04:43.660
four penguins in the original film seemed
especially enamoured with the magical nanny.

00:04:43.660 --> 00:04:45.800
"Anything for you, Mary Poppins."

00:04:45.800 --> 00:04:47.640
"You're our favourite person."

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:53.000
A new character we meet in the sequel is Mary's
cousin Topsy who runs a Fix-It shop that gets

00:04:53.000 --> 00:04:55.530
turned upside-down every second Wednesday.

00:04:55.530 --> 00:05:00.040
Her scenes with Mary, Jack and the children
feel like an homage to the first film's floating

00:05:00.040 --> 00:05:01.790
scene with Uncle Albert.

00:05:01.790 --> 00:05:06.200
And if you keep your eyes peeled, you'll see
the weather vane from the 1964 movie during

00:05:06.200 --> 00:05:08.840
the "Turning Turtle" song in Topsy's shop!

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:13.420
Interestingly, Meryl Streep's Topsy is a mix
of two characters from the books: Mary's cousin,

00:05:13.420 --> 00:05:17.840
Arthur Turvy, who Mary also asked to mend
a cracked Royal Doulton bowl, and a woman

00:05:17.840 --> 00:05:20.320
named Topsy who marries Arthur Turvy.

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:24.530
And, similar to the film, everything in Arthur
Turvy's life is the opposite of what it should

00:05:24.530 --> 00:05:26.330
be one day a month.

00:05:26.330 --> 00:05:31.090
Dick van Dyke makes a very special cameo in
Mary Poppins Returns as Mr Dawes Jr., which

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:36.220
is a little inside joke for fans of the original
movie because in that film he played Mr Dawes

00:05:36.220 --> 00:05:39.500
Sr, the father of the character he plays in
the sequel.

00:05:39.500 --> 00:05:44.610
And the amazing little dance the 93-year old
actor performs in the new movie is also reminiscent

00:05:44.610 --> 00:05:48.970
of some of Bert's famous dance moves from
the first film such as his dance alongside

00:05:48.970 --> 00:05:52.960
the animated penguins and his chimney sweep
routine on the rooftops of London.

00:05:52.960 --> 00:05:57.740
There's also a wink and a nod to his double-role
in the first movie when the sequel replays

00:05:57.740 --> 00:06:01.090
how he was credited for his second part as
Mr Dawes Sr.

00:06:01.090 --> 00:06:05.330
So, once again during the credits, when we
see Dick van Dyke's name, the letters first

00:06:05.330 --> 00:06:10.450
appear scrambled before they magically rearrange
themselves to reveal his real name.

00:06:10.450 --> 00:06:15.240
And when Dick van Dyke sits down to tell the
story about Michael's tuppence, he slyly starts

00:06:15.240 --> 00:06:19.700
by saying, "once upon a time, there was a
man with a wooden leg" which is a clever little

00:06:19.700 --> 00:06:22.920
call-back to the first film's running joke
about a wooden leg.

00:06:22.920 --> 00:06:25.580
"A wooden leg named Smith."

00:06:25.580 --> 00:06:31.090
In the 1964 movie, Dick van Dyke's Bert first
told the joke to Uncle Albert; then the Banks

00:06:31.090 --> 00:06:35.620
children passed it on to their father who
eventually told it to Dick van Dyke's Mr Dawes

00:06:35.620 --> 00:06:38.680
Sr, who later died laughing from the joke.

00:06:38.680 --> 00:06:43.110
As well as Dick van Dyke returning from the
first film, there's also a fun cameo by Karen

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:45.730
Dotrice who played the original Jane Banks.

00:06:45.730 --> 00:06:50.250
In Mary Poppins Returns, Dotrice turns up
on the street outside the Banks house asking

00:06:50.250 --> 00:06:54.500
for directions to 19 Cherry Tree Lane, which
is funny because that would have been her

00:06:54.500 --> 00:06:55.500
neighbour's house.

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:00.620
And when she leaves, Dotrice politely says,
'many thanks, sincerely', which is exactly

00:07:00.620 --> 00:07:05.490
how Jane and Michael signed off their advert
for a new nanny in the first film.

00:07:05.490 --> 00:07:09.639
Actor David Tomlinson who played Jane's father
is no longer alive, but this shot of a man

00:07:09.639 --> 00:07:13.990
in the park looks a lot like George Banks,
and the way the kite knocks his hat off, feels

00:07:13.990 --> 00:07:18.230
like a literal hat tip to the original movie
where Mr Banks had his bowler hat punched

00:07:18.230 --> 00:07:19.590
through when he was sacked.

00:07:19.590 --> 00:07:24.120
Also, look out for photographs of the original
Mr and Mrs Banks in the living room at 17

00:07:24.120 --> 00:07:25.120
Cherry Tree Lane.

00:07:25.120 --> 00:07:28.840
And there are plenty more delicious callbacks
to the original film.

00:07:28.840 --> 00:07:33.200
One of my favourites is when Mary arrives
at the Banks home and addresses the gobsmacked

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:34.200
Michael.

00:07:34.200 --> 00:07:35.200
"Mary..."

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:36.200
"Poppins!"

00:07:36.200 --> 00:07:37.660
"Close your mouth please Michael, we are still
not a codfish."

00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:39.810
"Jane Banks.

00:07:39.810 --> 00:07:41.500
Still rather inclined to giggle I see."

00:07:41.500 --> 00:07:42.700
"Close your mouth please Michael.

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:43.700
We are not codfish."

00:07:43.700 --> 00:07:48.220
"Now you, Jane."

00:07:48.220 --> 00:07:50.980
"Rather inclined to giggle.

00:07:50.980 --> 00:07:52.050
Doesn't put things away."

00:07:52.050 --> 00:07:56.190
Then Mary admires herself in the mirror and
walks away, although her reflection stays

00:07:56.190 --> 00:08:00.650
behind, watching and raising an eyebrow as
she leaves in a sweet throwback to when Mary's

00:08:00.650 --> 00:08:04.930
reflection took on a life of its own in the
first film, joining her in singing A Spoonful

00:08:04.930 --> 00:08:08.550
of Sugar, before watching Mary leave and then
winking!

00:08:08.550 --> 00:08:12.120
And then there's some classic moments from
the first film replayed with a twist such

00:08:12.120 --> 00:08:16.919
as Mary Poppins sliding up the bannisters,
as she did in the 1964 film, only this time

00:08:16.919 --> 00:08:19.389
Jane and Michael don't see her!

00:08:19.389 --> 00:08:23.490
Also Mary Poppins and the children tidy away
the mess made by Michael and Jane with some

00:08:23.490 --> 00:08:28.810
magical assistance, just as Mary did with
the young Michael and Jane in the first movie.

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:32.300
You might remember Miss Lark's dog Andrew
from the original movie.

00:08:32.300 --> 00:08:37.150
As Mary Poppins Returns is set around 25 years
after the first film, Miss Lark now has a

00:08:37.150 --> 00:08:41.360
new dog called Willoughby, which is a fun
little easter egg because in the books Miss

00:08:41.360 --> 00:08:44.449
Lark has two dogs, Andrew and Willoughby.

00:08:44.449 --> 00:08:48.220
And while Mary returning on a kite at the
start of the film is taken from PL Travers'

00:08:48.220 --> 00:08:52.720
second book, Mary Poppins Comes Back; the
fact she says she'll stay with the Banks family

00:08:52.720 --> 00:08:57.810
"till the door opens" comes from the third
book, "Mary Poppins Opens The Door."

00:08:57.810 --> 00:09:02.350
The kite also still has the Suffragette's
"Votes For Women" sash that Mrs Banks attached

00:09:02.350 --> 00:09:03.550
to it in the first movie.

00:09:03.550 --> 00:09:08.589
There are other intriguing easter eggs such
as the robin on Mary Poppins' hat, her talking

00:09:08.589 --> 00:09:13.389
parrot umbrella, and the St Paul's snow globe,
and I talk about why they're so important

00:09:13.389 --> 00:09:14.389
in my other video.

00:09:14.389 --> 00:09:18.680
I'll add a card with a link here as soon as
I upload it so subscribe and hit the bell

00:09:18.680 --> 00:09:21.180
to get notified as soon as it's posted!

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:24.839
Tap left to find out all the big differences
between the sequel and the original.

00:09:24.839 --> 00:09:27.459
Or tap right for my full Mary Poppins playlist.

00:09:27.459 --> 00:09:29.829
Thanks for watching and see ya next time!

00:09:29.829 --> 00:09:31.019
Yippee-ki-yay, movie lovers!

